[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says **one issue, one fix queued**. Its own table says two causes, and
its footnote names a third. This page reads the same export the desk's page reads and counts
as the desk counts: every report under its cause, and the one-day moves named on top. Each problem has a ticket of its own in
[umerme1/mega-training](https://github.com/umerme1/mega-training/issues).

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued fix is for the parser. It does nothing for the booking-v3 form hint, and nothing for
the one-day moves. Two of the three problems stay open after it ships.
</Callout>

## The three problems

<TicketTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

**parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day
first (`dayFirst` in `causes.ts` asks the platform, locale by locale), so `07/04` typed in the app
or sent by email means 7 April to them and 4 July to the parser. Reaches every tenant, through the
app, email and the booking-v2 form.

**hint.** Three tenants run booking-v3. Its form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field, so
their people type the month first as told, and the form then reads it the tenant's way, day
first. Same symptom, a flipped month, but a different bug in a different place. Only reports
that came in through the form from a booking-v3 tenant are this problem.

**one-day-move.** Five reports are not a month flip at all: the date moved by one day, an invite
came in before its booking, or two time zones got two different days. The desk read them as a
one-day move and wrote it in its notes; they stay counted under `parser`, where the desk filed
them, and get a ticket of their own on top.

## Why the merge hid this

- Reports were merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
  and all twelve subjects describe the symptom, not the cause. The same subject covers all three
  problems.
- Intake tagged each report from the reporter's browser locale, not from the tenant. The table
  above counts how many tags point at the wrong cause. The tag is a guess; tenant and channel
  are the fact.
- The export overlaps between pages, so 15 reports appear twice. `uniqueById` already handles
  it; the numbers here are deduplicated the same way.

## What to do with #4471

1. Keep #4471 for `parser` and link the parser ticket from it.
2. Open the `hint` and `one-day-move` tickets as their own work; they are filed and linked above.
3. Stop merging on subject alone during this incident: run `causeOf` on tenant and channel first,
   as the [dedupe explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) already does.

